      "pins": ["U1.2", "U3.2", "J3.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "POT"],
      "observed": "On the second sheet a wire (highlighted green) runs from U1 pin 2 (the LM1117 VO output, net +5V) down to U3 pin 2 (PB3, labelled POT) and to the op-amp's pin 3, so the +5V supply rail and the POT signal net are joined together. J3 pin 2 also carries the POT label, so the potentiometer wiper is tied straight to +5V.",
      "expected": "The POT net should connect only the potentiometer wiper (J3 pin 2) to the MCU analog input (U3 pin 2) and the op-amp input; the LM1117 output (+5V) must not be merged with it. The regulated +5V should feed only the logic supply and pull-ups (R3, R6), while the pot sense line remains a high-impedance analog input.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 LM1117, pin 2 VO tied to +5V; green wire continues from this node to U3 pin 2 (PB3) which carries the POT label (sheet /, page 2)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J3 POT: pin 2 -> POT (sheet /, page 1)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U3 ATtiny13V-10S: pin 2 PB3 -> POT (sheet /, page 2)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The potentiometer input is pinned to the 5V rail, so the MCU ADC always reads full scale and the fan-speed-from-pot control is lost; the pot wiper also fights the regulator, potentially over-currenting the LM1117 and the pot.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing is mostly a standard ATtiny fan controller (PMOS high-side driver, BC817, tach pull-up, reset pull-up, 100nF decoupling, LM1117 generating +5V from VCC), but a wire on the second sheet ties the LM1117 +5V output directly to the POT net (U3 pin 2 / J3 pin 2), shorting the potentiometer analog input to the supply rail."
}

